\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The deployed dataflow, with every edge coloured by what it carries. The architectural half of this section's argument is a code fact and is drawn here rather than described: \texttt  {GraspPointHead.forward} takes $(\text  {uv}, \text  {conf}, \text  {proprio}, \text  {box\_emb}, \text  {frame\_emb})$ and \emph  {no text argument}, so no red edge reaches object selection, approach or grasping. Every red edge terminates in one $(x,y)$ latched once by \texttt  {set\_place(place\_head(command\_emb))} inside \texttt  {MicroVLAPolicy.reset()} and never re-read --- which also answers whether re-latching mid-episode could change the swap result: there is no re-latch.}}{18}{figure.caption.36}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dataflow}{{7}{18}{The deployed dataflow, with every edge coloured by what it carries. The architectural half of this section's argument is a code fact and is drawn here rather than described: \texttt {GraspPointHead.forward} takes $(\text {uv}, \text {conf}, \text {proprio}, \text {box\_emb}, \text {frame\_emb})$ and \emph {no text argument}, so no red edge reaches object selection, approach or grasping. Every red edge terminates in one $(x,y)$ latched once by \texttt {set\_place(place\_head(command\_emb))} inside \texttt {MicroVLAPolicy.reset()} and never re-read --- which also answers whether re-latching mid-episode could change the swap result: there is no re-latch}{figure.caption.36}{}}
